\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{Limitations}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The limitations are relative to the purpose in
[Motivation and aim](fnip-0020).}

\p{The notes contain the Introduction and two further chapters.
[State variables and phase space](fnip-0002) contains the Morris–Lecar
pair, the trajectory, and one voltage trace.
[[fnip-0026]] is, in what is written, local existence and uniqueness
for a continuously differentiable velocity, and the linearization at
an equilibrium. The chapters from the
conductance-based model onward are absent
because several of their sources are absent: Dayan and Abbott,
“Theoretical Neuroscience” (2001); Nicholls, Martin, Fuchs, Brown,
Diamond, and Weisblat, “From Neuron to Brain” (2012); Claycomb and
Tran, “Introductory Biophysics” (2011); and the 2006 edition of
Carnevale and Hines, “The NEURON Book”. The cost is that the notes
have no derivation of the conductance-based model and no account of the
membrane, the circuits, or the development that later chapters would
use those books to supply.}

\p{Volume Two, the algorithmic reading named in
[How to read these notes](fnip-0023), is absent. The cost is that a
code, a temporal memory, and a sensorimotor rule have no chapter here.}

\p{The network sentence in [Motivation and aim](fnip-0020), that
ongoing activity is a background process into which a stimulus arrives,
remains a view. The books cited there treat single model neurons.
Deco, Jirsa, and McIntosh (2011) and Arieli, Sterkin, Grinvald, and
Aertsen, Science 273 (1996), are the sources that would let that
sentence stand as evidence, and neither is in hand. The cost is that
the notes make no network claim.}

\p{A result in these notes is a proved statement about a dynamical
system or a calculation in a cited model. A proposal is marked as the
view that carries it. No statement in the notes fixes a biological
mechanism, a parameter value of a living cell, or a clinical claim.}
